ch->dma.desc++;
ch->dma.desc %= LTQ_DESC_NUM;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the pointer of the next descriptor is incorrectly assigned to the same SKB as the current descriptor, leading to two descriptors referencing the same SKB. This results in memory corruption when the system attempts to insert a second packet into an already occupied buffer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
struct sk_buff *skb = ch->skb[ch->dma.desc];
ch->skb[ch->dma.desc] = skb;
```
Reason:
The fix correctly saves the current SKB pointer before attempting to allocate a new SKB. If the allocation fails, it restores the original SKB pointer, ensuring that the current descriptor does not end up pointing to the same SKB as the next descriptor, thus preventing memory corruption.

